  2.  Getting started

  2.1. Finding documentation

  All current Acton Research Corp. Monochromators ship with a cd that
  contains the manual for the instrument. They are provided in Adobe
  pdf format and explain how to setup, use, and talk to the instrument.
  Additional information can be provided by support@acton-research.com.
  The phone number for tech support and sales is 978-263-3584. 

  2.2. Setting up your Instrument

  Though many instruments are similar each instrument is unique in how
  it is going to be used and setup. Always consult the manual and any 
  provided documentation before setting up the instrument. The provided
  documentation will always preceed any instructions found in this
  HOWTO.
 
  The normal steps for setting up an instrument are as follows ...
      o Remove instrument from shipping box (SAVE THE BOX, Acton 
        Research Corp. will not service instruments returned in
        non-approved packaging. This is for your own protection. 
        To many instruments have arrived back at Acton a blobs
        of metal and shattered glass).
      o Setup instrument in the experiment you are going to be working
        with. (Refer to provided documentation) This often involves
        removing shipping screws, shipping covers, and the divertor
        shipping screws (Big red screws under each side slit if the
        option was purchased). On the more advanced instruments it
        may also require the instalation of the Grating Turret.
        (REFER TO PROVIDED DOCUMENTATION, if you have questions feel
        free to call us at 978-263-3584).
      o Attach a serial cable from the instrument to you computer. The
        instrument shipped with a 9 pin to 9 pin Serial cable. If you
        have a different configuration such as a SGI or MAC a good
        source of reference is the Serial Programming Guide for 
        POSIX Operating Systems. It contains wiring for various serial
        ports.
      o Verify the instrument is alive by talking to it through 
        terminal programs such as Kermit or MiniTerm.
 
  2.3. Talking to the instrument through Kermit

  Kermit is a simple communications protocol that allows one to talk to
  serial ports on almost every operating system ever created. It is hosted 
  by Columbia University at http://www.kermit-project.org/. 

  Once kermit is invoked it a very simple matter to connect to an Acton
  Monochromator. 
       1. Determine the line for the serial port. Under x86 Linux the
       device drivers use the /dev/ttyS* notation where * is the Serial 
       Port number (the First Port is 0, Second 1, ect.)
       
       C-Kermit>set line /dev/ttyS0

       2. Set the speed to 9600 baud.

       C-Kermit>set speed 9600

       3. Turn off carrier watch
  
       C-Kermit>set carrier-watch off			

       4. Type 'C' to enter terminal mode

       C-Kermit>C
       Connecting to /dev/ttyS0, speed 9600.
       The escape character is Ctrl-\ (ASCII 28, FS)
       Type the escape character followed by C to get back,
       or followed by ? to see other options.
       ----------------------------------------------------

       5. You are now talking directly to the device. A couple of comands 
       to try are as follows
          who <CR> - returns the Instrument ID
          ?nm <CR> - returns the wavelength of the instrument
          100.0 goto <CR> - sends the instrument to 100.0 nm
          ?grating <CR> - returns which grating is selected
          ?gratings <CR> - returns a list of available gratings
          1 grating <CR> - sets the current grating to grating 1

       6. To exit, CTRL-\ C
       CTRL-\C
       C-Kermit>quit
       %
 
  3.  Programming examples
	
  Acton Research Monochromators are serial devices that work through the
  computers RS-232 port. It is strongly recomended you read the 
  Linux Serial-HOWTO, Serial Programming HOWTO, and Serial Programming 
  Guide for POSIX Compliant Operating Systems before proceding.

  3.1 Comand Format

  Communications with Acton Research Monochromators take the form of 
  sending a command terminated with a Carriage Return. This is "\r" 
  in C speak. In C "\n" sends a Carriage Return , Line Feed. Acton
  Research Monochromators treat the Line Feed as a garbage character.
  Do not send "\n" to the instrument. If the monochromator accepts the
  command it will respond with a "ok\r". If the monochomator rejects the
  command it will respond with a "?\r". When working with the responces
  from the monochromator be aware that the monochromator echo's characters
  sent to it. You need to take this into account when processing strings i
  from the monochromator.

  The commands are documented in the documentation provided with the 
  instrument.

  3.2 Some Example code

  /* Test Code ...
  /*    The code provides an example of how to open a serial port and query the
  /*    wavelength of an Acton Research Corporation (ARC) Monochromator.
  /*
  /*    Things to note :
  /*              1. ARC Monochromator's recognize valid commands with a " ok\r"
  /*              2. ARC Monochromator's acknowledge in-valid commands with
  /*                 a " ?\r".
  /*              3. Commands sent to the monochromator will not be processed
  /*                 until a Carriage Return ( "\r" ) is sent.
  /*              4. Never send a LineFeed to the instrument ( "\n" ).
  /*              5. This example assumes we are using the first port on a linux
  /*                 system "/dev/ttyS0".
  /*              6. Monochromator's echo all characters sent to them.
  /*
  /*    Code based on examples provided in :
  /*         Serial Programming HOWTO,
  /*            http://www.linuxdoc.org/HOWTO/Serial-Programming-HOWTO/x115.html
  /*         Serial Programming Guide for POSIX Operating Systems,
  /*              http://www.easysw.com/~mike/serial/serial.html#2_5
  /*    For further reading :
  /*         Serial HOWTO,
  /*              http://www.linuxdoc.org/HOWTO/Serial-HOWTO.html
  /*         Linux I/O port programming mini-HOWTO,
  /*              http://www.linuxdoc.org/HOWTO/mini/IO-Port-Programming.html
  */
  #include <sys/types.h>
  #include <sys/stat.h>
  #include <fcntl.h>
  #include <termios.h>
  #include <stdio.h>

  /* baudrate settings are defined in <asm/termbits.h>, which is included by <termios.h> */
  /* Acton Research Monochromator's
  /*       Communicate at 9600 Baud, 8 Bits, No Parity, 1 Stop Bit */
  #define BAUDRATE B9600
  /* change this definition for the correct port */
  #define MODEMDEVICE "/dev/ttyS0"
  #define _POSIX_SOURCE 1 /* POSIX compliant source */
  #define FALSE 0
  #define TRUE 1

  volatile int STOP=FALSE;

  int main(int argc, char **argv)
  {
  int fd; /* Pointer to Serial Device */
  int res;
  struct termios oldtio,newtio;
  char buf[255];

  /*
    Open modem device for reading and writing and not as controlling tty
    because we don't want to get killed if linenoise sends CTRL-C.
  */
  fd = open(MODEMDEVICE, O_RDWR | O_NOCTTY );
  if (fd <0) {perror(MODEMDEVICE); exit(-1); }

  tcgetattr(fd,&oldtio); /* save current serial port settings */
  bzero(&newtio, sizeof(newtio)); /* clear struct for new port settings */

  /*
  BAUDRATE: Set bps rate. You could also use cfsetispeed and cfsetospeed.
  CRTSCTS : output hardware flow control (only used if the cable has
            all necessary lines. See sect. 7 of Serial-HOWTO)
  CS8     : 8n1 (8bit,no parity,1 stopbit)
  CLOCAL  : local connection, no modem contol
  CREAD   : enable receiving characters
  */
  newtio.c_cflag = BAUDRATE | CRTSCTS | CS8 | CLOCAL | CREAD;

  /*
  IGNPAR  : ignore bytes with parity errors
  ICRNL   : map CR to NL (otherwise a CR input on the other computer
            will not terminate input)
            otherwise make device raw (no other input processing)
  */
  newtio.c_iflag = IGNPAR | ICRNL;

  /*
  Raw output.
  */
  newtio.c_lflag = ~(ICANON | ECHO | ECHOE | ISIG);

  /*
    now clean the modem line and activate the settings for the port
  */
  tcflush(fd, TCIFLUSH);
  tcsetattr(fd,TCSANOW,&newtio);

  /* Serial port is now opened and set up */

  strcpy(buf,"?nm\r"); /* Send "?nm" command to the Monochromator */
  write(fd,buf,4);

  /* wait for the monochromator to ackknowledge by sending back an "ok" */
  while (STOP==FALSE) {     /* loop until we have a terminating condition */
         /* read blocks program execution until a line terminating character is
            input, even if more than 255 chars are input. If the number
            of characters read is smaller than the number of chars available,
            subsequent reads will return the remaining chars. res will be set
            to the actual number of characters actually read */
      res = read(fd,buf,1);
      buf[res]=0;             /* set end of string, so we can printf */
      if (res > 0)            /* output command results to the console */
         { printf("%s", buf); }
      if (buf[0]=='k') STOP=TRUE;
   } 
  printf("\n");

  /* restore the old port settings */
  tcsetattr(fd,TCSANOW,&oldtio);
  }

